HHIISSTTOORRYY
       The Linux ssttaattffss() was inspired by the 4.4BSD one (but they do not  use
       the same structure).

       The  original  Linux  ssttaattffss()  and ffssttaattffss() system calls were not de‐
       signed with extremely large file sizes in  mind.   Subsequently,  Linux
       2.6 added new ssttaattffss6644() and ffssttaattffss6644() system calls that employ a new
       structure, _s_t_a_t_f_s_6_4.  The new structure contains the same fields as the
       original  _s_t_a_t_f_s  structure,  but  the  sizes of various fields are in‐
       creased, to accommodate large file sizes.  The glibc ssttaattffss()  and  ffss‐‐
       ttaattffss()  wrapper  functions  transparently deal with the kernel differ‐
       ences.

       LSB has deprecated the library calls ssttaattffss() and ffssttaattffss()  and  tells
       us to use ssttaattvvffss(3) and ffssttaattvvffss(3) instead.

